    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Singer sits at 148 feet above sea level on gently rolling Piney Woods terrain, where sandy soils and forested uplands shape local drainage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/singer_louisiana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Singer emerged during the late 19th‑century lumber boom, drawing workers to sawmills that expanded from local needs to national markets by 1900.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/singer_louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1912 Singer briefly served as Beauregard Parish’s first seat before DeRidder won the vote with 663 to Singer’s 434.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/singer_louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Singer experiences a humid subtropical climate, with hot, humid summers and mild winters that never bring a dry season.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/singer_louisiana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Singer’s Gulf‑Coast location makes it vulnerable to hurricanes, as Hurricane Laura in 2020 delivered winds and over 11 inches of rain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/singer_louisiana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "After WWII, Singer’s timber industry shifted toward sustainable pine plantations, oil and gas, and beef cattle farming as forests thinned.",
